# Coolify's bundled proxy issues its OWN Let's Encrypt wildcard for
# *.thefipster.de via the netcup DNS-01 challenge — the same mechanism and the
# same credentials as Traefik on the infra VM, but a separate certificate and a
# separate ACME account. Nothing is exposed to the internet either way.
#
# COOLIFY DOES NOT READ THIS FILE, and it has no credentials form to type them
# into either. They go into the proxy's own docker-compose, edited in the UI at
# Servers -> your server -> Proxy -> Configuration, as an `environment:` block
# on the traefik service — alongside the flags that switch the challenge from
# Coolify's default HTTP-01 (which cannot issue a wildcard at all) to netcup
# DNS-01. Full procedure: docs/coolify-setup.md step 4.
#
# So this file is the RECOVERY COPY, not merely documentation. Coolify
# regenerates that compose from its own store, and has been reported to discard
# custom proxy edits on server revalidation; when it does, these are the values
# you put back. Seeded by scripts/init-coolify.sh, gitignored like every
# other .env.
#
# Same netcup caveats as infra/traefik/.env: propagation is slow (~10 min, hence
# NETCUP_PROPAGATION_TIMEOUT=900 in the proxy compose), and a wildcard with no
# apex SAN avoids two TXT records racing at the same _acme-challenge FQDN.
# See docs/traefik-setup.md.

NETCUP_CUSTOMER_NUMBER=
NETCUP_API_KEY=
NETCUP_API_PASSWORD=
